(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-lipid-fibrates-niacin-quiz"]={"title":"Fibrates, Niacin & Lipid Profiles Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In which patient is a fibrate contraindicated?","choices":["One with triglycerides over 1000 mg/dL","One with a low HDL cholesterol","One with well-controlled diabetes","One with existing gallbladder disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fibrate contraindications are pregnancy, severe hepatic or renal dysfunction, and existing gallbladder disease; cholelithiasis is also an adverse effect. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is characteristic of fibrates?","choices":["Cholelithiasis","Cutaneous flushing","Vitamin malabsorption","Hypersensitivity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrate adverse effects are gastrointestinal (nausea, abdominal pain, diarrhea), cholelithiasis and myopathy. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A patient's only lipid problem is a triglyceride level over 1000 mg/dL. Which class is most appropriate?","choices":["A bile acid sequestrant","A fibrate","Ezetimibe","A PCSK9 inhibitor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Triglycerides over 1000 mg/dL are the primary fibrate indication; fibrates lower triglycerides by 20 to 50% (niacin by a similar amount). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which substance is listed as a niacin interaction?","choices":["Antacids","Alcohol","Warfarin","Thyroxine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Niacin interactions are statins, bile acid sequestrants and alcohol. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How do fibrates lower triglycerides?","choices":["More fatty acid oxidation, less VLDL","Less fatty acid release from fat","Binding bile acids in the gut","Blocking cholesterol absorption"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Increased fatty acid oxidation reduces secretion of triglyceride-rich VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein), lowering triglycerides by 20 to 50%. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What does niacin do to glucose control at larger doses?","choices":["Improves glucose tolerance","Masks hypoglycemia","Decreases glucose tolerance","Has no glucose effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Larger doses raise glucose and decrease glucose tolerance, making diabetes a relative contraindication. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which lipid-lowering class can RAISE triglycerides?","choices":["Fibric acid derivatives","Bile acid sequestrants","Nicotinic acid (niacin)","HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Resins show no change or a rise in triglycerides, because they may increase VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) production. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which class lowers LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol the most?","choices":["Fibrates","Niacin","Statins","Ezetimibe"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Statins lower LDL by 18 to 55%, the most of any class; they are the most efficacious and first line. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How does niacin reduce hepatic VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) production?","choices":["Less fatty acid mobilized from fat","More fatty acid oxidation","Binding of bile acids","Blocking cholesterol uptake"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Niacin decreases mobilization of free fatty acids from adipose tissue, so the liver makes less triglyceride, VLDL and apo B; LDL falls and HDL rises. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to niacin?","choices":["Peptic ulcer disease","Chronic liver disease","History of symptomatic gout","Diabetes with glucose intolerance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic liver disease is the absolute contraindication; peptic ulcer disease, symptomatic gout, significant hyperuricemia and diabetes are relative ones. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A patient with high triglycerides has existing gallbladder disease. Which agent should be avoided?","choices":["Niacin","Atorvastatin","Fenofibrate","Evolocumab"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Existing gallbladder disease contraindicates fibrates, which cause cholelithiasis. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which nuclear transcription factor do fibrates activate?","choices":["PPAR-alpha","The LDL receptor","HMG-CoA reductase","PCSK9"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates activate PPAR-alpha, increasing fatty acid oxidation (so less VLDL, very low-density lipoprotein, is secreted) and increasing ApoA-1 expression. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"For which lipid pattern is niacin particularly useful?","choices":["Isolated high HDL","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Pregnancy-related high LDL","Atherogenic dyslipidemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Niacin is useful for atherogenic dyslipidemia and as combination therapy when atherogenic dyslipidemia comes with elevated LDL (low-density lipoprotein). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the primary indication for a fibrate?","choices":["LDL over 190 in a young adult","High LDL during pregnancy","Statin-induced raised enzymes","Triglycerides over 1000 or low HDL"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The primary indication is triglycerides above 1000 mg/dL or low HDL (high-density lipoprotein) cholesterol. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 39"}]};
